1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,650
A film presents its scenes in an edited order.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:08,150
In a game, even the first person you speak to can be your choice.

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:13,610
The official Divinity: Original Sin 2 footage
shows several answers during a conversation.

4
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:17,950
Good sentences alone do not define what happens after each choice.

5
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:22,530
In the authoring tool, a question and its action options are edited separately.

6
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:28,165
This shows event construction, rather than
proving every condition in the finished game.

7
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:33,610
We built a small story game called The Harbor Seal to test the problem.

8
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,470
From this point, this screen is our own playable prototype.

9
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:40,750
Going straight to the harbor guard means we
have neither the item nor the information yet.

10
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:43,590
His first response must start from that state.

11
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:46,250
This time we read the notice first and come back.

12
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:50,330
The place and character are unchanged, but
the facts the player brings are different.

13
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:55,690
The next line should check what is true now before assuming a scene number.

14
00:00:55,760 --> 00:00:57,440
Let us draw that distinction.

15
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:00,857
We first ask about a rule we do not know.

16
00:01:01,107 --> 00:01:06,237
When we speak again, the guard continues the
conversation instead of repeating the first explanation.

17
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:09,577
Compare the first greeting with the response after the explanation.

18
00:01:09,707 --> 00:01:13,767
What was said matters more than the number of visits.

19
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:18,273
The first principle is to write order and state separately.

20
00:01:18,463 --> 00:01:22,913
Meeting the guard second does not mean the player knows the clue.

21
00:01:23,103 --> 00:01:26,773
The script on the left lists scene one and scene two.

22
00:01:26,823 --> 00:01:32,393
The one on the right checks whether the clue was
seen, the item is held, and the companion is present.

23
00:01:32,563 --> 00:01:35,833
This does not require a new story for every combination.

24
00:01:36,023 --> 00:01:39,733
Write only the conditions needed for this particular line to be true.

25
00:01:39,843 --> 00:01:43,388
For example, an uninformed player asks a question. An informed player can

26
00:01:43,403 --> 00:01:47,508
answer using the clue. The character can keep the same voice and goal.

27
00:01:47,523 --> 00:01:51,428
Keep the condition for entering a scene separate
from the condition for choosing a line.

28
00:01:51,443 --> 00:01:55,843
Combining them can make a small edit change an unexpected branch.

29
00:01:59,550 --> 00:02:04,160
In this official Divinity conversation, the
speaker addresses the protagonist by name.

30
00:02:04,270 --> 00:02:08,260
What we can observe is that recognition is expressed in the dialogue.

31
00:02:08,510 --> 00:02:12,220
This short clip cannot establish how the recognition condition is implemented.

32
00:02:12,390 --> 00:02:16,460
We test who actually receives the information in our own game.

33
00:02:16,630 --> 00:02:19,495
The notice explains the rule for proving passage.

34
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:24,160
Reading it does not automatically tell the guard that the player has visited it.

35
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:27,035
We choose the answer that mentions the rule.

36
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:30,740
That action records the information shared with the guard.

37
00:02:30,970 --> 00:02:35,100
Talking again now continues the earlier conversation
instead of asking the introductory question.

38
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:39,100
Reading and sharing were implemented as different events.

39
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:42,600
We also try reading the record before meeting the guard.

40
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:47,800
Whatever the visit order, a character should
not pretend to know an unshared fact.

41
00:02:48,010 --> 00:02:54,620
When a line sounds wrong, trace when that character
learned the fact before rewriting the sentence.

42
00:02:55,633 --> 00:02:59,243
The second principle is to distinguish the owner of information.

43
00:02:59,433 --> 00:03:04,743
A world event, a fact seen by the player, and a fact
shared with another character are separate records.

44
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:08,558
Three separate columns expose mistakes. A locked door is a

45
00:03:08,573 --> 00:03:12,463
world state. Reading why it was locked is player knowledge.

46
00:03:12,673 --> 00:03:16,623
Only telling the guard makes it shared information.

47
00:03:16,813 --> 00:03:21,078
Until then, a line such as I know you read
the notice would need another explanation.

48
00:03:21,093 --> 00:03:27,523
Start with small facts needed by the dialogue: knowing
a name, hearing a promise, or asking a question before.

49
00:03:27,753 --> 00:03:30,683
There is no need to record every irrelevant action.

50
00:03:30,773 --> 00:03:35,123
On repeat conversations, retire answered questions
and keep the replies that still make sense.

51
00:03:35,233 --> 00:03:38,353
Smooth writing is built on those conditions.

52
00:03:42,067 --> 00:03:48,837
The official Divinity authoring demonstration creates a ring,
changes its name and description, and puts it into an inventory.

53
00:03:49,047 --> 00:03:52,657
This is actual tool work preparing an item for a story.

54
00:03:52,807 --> 00:03:57,077
We do not invent a later plot consequence that this footage does not show.

55
00:03:57,107 --> 00:04:00,597
In our harbor test, a companion can hold the seal.

56
00:04:00,667 --> 00:04:05,557
An item being somewhere in the party is different
from the protagonist being able to present it now.

57
00:04:05,767 --> 00:04:08,457
We pass the seal to the companion and speak with the guard.

58
00:04:08,627 --> 00:04:12,052
While the companion is present, an answer lets that companion show it.

59
00:04:12,067 --> 00:04:15,197
This time we let the companion rest and return alone.

60
00:04:15,487 --> 00:04:20,237
A line claiming the protagonist takes the seal
from a pocket would contradict its current owner.

61
00:04:20,507 --> 00:04:26,177
The test checks the owner again, removes that
answer, and keeps a way to recover the seal.

62
00:04:26,607 --> 00:04:29,757
We continue by meeting the companion and taking it back.

63
00:04:30,107 --> 00:04:33,752
When turning prose into game dialogue, remember that held it,

64
00:04:33,767 --> 00:04:37,057
handed it over, and came together all require an actual state.

65
00:04:37,983 --> 00:04:41,773
This time we call the companion back instead of recovering the seal.

66
00:04:41,923 --> 00:04:46,873
The companion still owns it, but the option
to show it returns when they are present.

67
00:04:47,023 --> 00:04:51,073
Compare the replies while the companion is absent and after they return.

68
00:04:51,263 --> 00:04:54,943
Ownership and current access require separate checks.

69
00:04:55,667 --> 00:05:00,517
The third principle is to distinguish historical facts from current facts.

70
00:05:00,787 --> 00:05:04,357
Having acquired the seal earlier does not establish its current owner.

71
00:05:04,667 --> 00:05:08,957
In the diagram, the item moves between the protagonist, companion, and storage.

72
00:05:08,987 --> 00:05:12,557
Its acquisition remains in history while its owner and location change.

73
00:05:12,767 --> 00:05:17,192
Write which fact a line requires. Remembering an earlier discovery needs

74
00:05:17,207 --> 00:05:21,852
history. Offering to show the item now needs present ownership and access.

75
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:28,077
If a character can leave, inspect essential
information and items held only by that character.

76
00:05:28,367 --> 00:05:31,717
Provide a fitting alternative source or recovery path.

77
00:05:32,007 --> 00:05:35,352
Rather than removing freedom to prevent every exception, check that

78
00:05:35,367 --> 00:05:39,107
permitted changes are reflected consistently in later words and actions.

79
00:05:42,817 --> 00:05:49,607
An event card in the Divinity authoring demonstration lists distinct
actions such as attacking a wolf, feeding it, and running away.

80
00:05:49,757 --> 00:05:53,007
Different labels do not automatically produce different consequences.

81
00:05:53,237 --> 00:05:56,907
The visible editing operation and the design
of its outcomes must be considered separately.

82
00:05:57,097 --> 00:06:00,127
Our own game gives three answers on the road to the harbor:

83
00:06:00,397 --> 00:06:04,527
give food, take a detour, or calmly drive the animal away.

84
00:06:04,677 --> 00:06:09,422
Giving food uses a ration. Taking the detour takes more time. All three can

85
00:06:09,437 --> 00:06:13,527
reach the harbor, while resources and recorded events remain different.

86
00:06:13,737 --> 00:06:18,327
We run the feeding route, then start a fresh run for the detour.

87
00:06:18,497 --> 00:06:22,367
A later line mentioning the journey changes to match the result.

88
00:06:22,657 --> 00:06:29,027
Instead of building three enormous maps, the branches rejoin
the same scene while carrying traces of their choices.

89
00:06:29,117 --> 00:06:32,387
The question is whether the next statement about what

90
00:06:32,597 --> 00:06:36,177
this person did agrees with their actual action.

91
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:39,070
Let us try the third option.

92
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:45,370
After we make the animal move aside, the guard
mentions that action instead of saying we fed it.

93
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:49,790
The same character changes one sentence when the earlier action changes.

94
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:52,980
A shared destination does not have to erase the choice.

95
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:59,330
The fourth principle is to define both common conditions
and retained differences when branches rejoin.

96
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:02,030
Reaching the harbor is shared.

97
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:05,990
Whether food was used or a detour taken can remain recorded.

98
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:10,970
Even within the same scene, a short response or available answer can differ.

99
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:15,590
A line celebrating a victory requires that victory to have happened.

100
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:20,025
A triumphant sentence after a failed encounter breaks the connection.

101
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:23,850
Winning and reaching the next area may be different facts.

102
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:28,250
If defeat leads to rescue and continued progress, write lines for that route.

103
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:31,450
The goal is not to maximize the branch count.

104
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:35,870
It is to keep the later story from contradicting an action the game allowed.

105
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:44,690
The Divinity authoring tools prepare map locations and event cards separately.

106
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:49,870
Seeing a location and hearing its explanation can be treated as separate facts.

107
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:54,250
In our test, we skip the opening explanation and go straight to the harbor.

108
00:07:54,500 --> 00:07:58,790
The immediate action should still be understandable
without reading the long background.

109
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:01,830
The guard asks for a seal he has not received.

110
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:06,785
The conversation still explains what is needed and where it can be checked.

111
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:10,910
Opening the notice lets the player recover the facts needed at this stage.

112
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:14,690
The game does not force an answer that assumes unfamiliar history.

113
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,230
We also run the route that read the explanation.

114
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:21,750
Previously heard material is not repeated;
a short answer confirms the next action.

115
00:08:21,980 --> 00:08:25,450
This is not a license to insert arbitrary exposition.

116
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:29,810
The setting must explain why this place and
character can provide that information.

117
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:36,530
Recoverable essential facts and optional
background give each line a clearer purpose.

118
00:08:37,483 --> 00:08:40,833
After handing over the seal, we talk to the guard again.

119
00:08:41,003 --> 00:08:45,793
The reply now invites us inside instead of demanding the same object.

120
00:08:46,023 --> 00:08:49,773
We also check whether the conversation remembers a completed event.

121
00:08:49,923 --> 00:08:55,003
Asking again just because the object left
our hands would undo the story progress.

122
00:08:55,733 --> 00:09:00,343
We now distinguish facts required for
progress from background that adds richness.

123
00:09:00,553 --> 00:09:03,723
We classify their role, not just their length.

124
00:09:03,953 --> 00:09:06,563
The condition for entering the harbor is essential.

125
00:09:06,633 --> 00:09:10,603
An incident from the guard’s childhood may be optional background.

126
00:09:11,073 --> 00:09:17,663
If an essential fact exists only in a one-time scene,
skipping that scene can erase the reason for the next action.

127
00:09:17,933 --> 00:09:20,223
Provide a natural way to check it again.

128
00:09:20,393 --> 00:09:23,838
There is no need to repeat everything to a player who read it.

129
00:09:23,853 --> 00:09:28,263
Offer a brief confirmation to informed players
and the necessary explanation to others.

130
00:09:28,453 --> 00:09:32,598
This concerns missing or reordered story information during play:

131
00:09:32,613 --> 00:09:37,093
keeping a valid basis for dialogue throughout the narrative.

132
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:44,970
We now change the dialogue conditions directly in our own story game.

133
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:48,810
Finishing the normal route once can miss important combinations.

134
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:53,430
In the first run, we read the notice, acquire the seal, and meet the guard.

135
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:56,910
Handing over the required item opens the next scene.

136
00:09:57,140 --> 00:10:00,190
In the second run, we skip the explanation and meet the guard first.

137
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:05,890
We check that the dialogue does not claim unlearned
knowledge and that information remains accessible.

138
00:10:06,060 --> 00:10:09,045
In the third run, we leave the seal with a resting companion.

139
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:14,330
We compare the unavailable answer with its return after retrieving the item.

140
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:17,110
In the fourth run, we make different road choices.

141
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:21,670
We check that rejoining the harbor does not
overwrite rations or the journey record.

142
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:26,510
Each run records the action, changed facts, and selected dialogue together.

143
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:31,570
When something fails, the connection can be traced in that record.

144
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:34,865
This test does not judge story enjoyment in place of people.

145
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:39,680
It is a small technical check for
contradictions between writing and actual play.

146
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:44,370
A game script needs both its sentences and the conditions that make them valid.

147
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:48,670
Record who knows what, who holds what, and which actions occurred.

148
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:51,790
Separate order from state and distinguish who owns information.

149
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:54,830
Do not confuse past acquisition with present possession.

150
00:10:55,240 --> 00:11:00,630
Keep traces of actions when branches rejoin, and
let players recover essential facts they missed.

151
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,130
Finally, play through different orders and ownership states.

152
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:09,945
Logs help locate contradictions; human play
review evaluates understanding and emotion.

153
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:14,665
Add one line beside the dialogue you are writing: what must happen first for

154
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:19,070
this statement to be true? That question connects game writing to actual play.
